/* exports_cuda.map — what liblorrax_ffi.so keeps PRIVATE.
 *
 * A linker version script (ld --version-script).  Everything matched by
 * `local:` is demoted to a local symbol and disappears from `.dynsym`;
 * references to it from inside this library bind at static-link time, so it
 * can be neither interposed by, nor interpose on, anything else in the
 * process.  Everything not matched keeps default visibility.
 *
 * WHAT THIS CLOSES — KNOWN_FAILURES L1, the cross-.so one-definition-rule
 * violation.  liblorrax_ffi.so and liblorrax_ffi_host.so are both dlopened
 * RTLD_GLOBAL into one process (a CUDA-capable process legitimately does
 * both: CUDA handlers on the device, host phdf5 reads on the CPU backend),
 * and ld.so resolves a name to the FIRST object that defined it -- for the
 * whole process, INCLUDING for the later library's own internal calls.
 *
 * MEASURED 2026-08-07 on the BUILD_NOTES-pinned pair, `nm -D --defined-only`:
 * 259 names in common, of which 25 are LORRAX's own -- the nine `lrx_*`
 * ctypes entry points plus sixteen mangled `lorrax_ffi::phdf5::*`
 * (`open_ctx`, `close_ctx`, `ensure_dataset`, `open_dataset_ro`,
 * `ensure_pinned`, `ensure_read_buf`, `ensure_mpi_initialized`, `env_flag`,
 * `~PhdfCtx` x2, the `dt::` HDF5 type singletons and their guards).  Those 25
 * are the defect: `PhdfCtx` has a DIFFERENT LAYOUT in the two builds, so one
 * library's handler read the other's struct at the wrong offsets, which is
 * B1's `offset_base=[0,0,0,4596944070643295330]` -- a float64 as an int64.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * WHY THIS HIDES `*lorrax_ffi*` AND NOT `*`.  A DENYLIST, DELIBERATELY.
 * ============================================================================
 * The obvious version of this file is `local: *;` with the handler surface
 * and the ctypes ABI listed under `global:` -- "export only the sanctioned
 * surface".  IT WAS WRITTEN THAT WAY FIRST AND IT CRASHES, and the crash is
 * the reason this file has the shape it has.
 *
 * MEASURED, Perlmutter 2026-08-08, same tree, same commands, only the host
 * .so's version script moving (`services/distrib_la/tests/
 * test_distrib_la_contract.py -k "slate and cpu"`, artifacts under
 * /pscratch/sd/j/jackm/svc_ffi_odr/_reports/slate_*.xml):
 *
 *   version script            slate host cells   what stayed global
 *   ------------------------  -----------------  --------------------------
 *   none at all                8 P / 2 skip      everything
 *   this one (*lorrax_ffi*)    8 P / 2 skip      all third-party
 *   `local: *` + _ZT* global   3 P / 5 CRASH     typeinfo/typeinfo-name/vtable
 *   `local: *` + slate/blas/
 *      std templates global    8 P / 2 skip      third-party TEMPLATES
 *   `local: *`                 3 P / 5 CRASH     nothing
 *
 * The two crashing arms are exactly the two that localise the SLATE / blaspp
 * / libstdc++ TEMPLATE INSTANTIATIONS our own translation units emit;
 * localising the typeinfo and vtables is harmless, and localising the
 * templates is fatal on its own.  `SlatePotrfHostFfi` segfaults inside the
 * handler (`Fatal Python error: Segmentation fault`, top frame
 * `distrib_la/_slate.py:344 _potrf`), five cells:
 * slate_cholesky_trsm_cpu[float64,complex128],
 * slate_trsm_rectangular_rhs_cpu[16,48], slate_batched_cholesky_trsm_cpu.
 *
 * That is the vague-linkage contract, and it is not ours to break.  Our TUs
 * emit weak COMDAT copies of SLATE's inline and template code; the dynamic
 * linker is SUPPOSED to merge them with libslate.so's, so that one
 * definition -- and one copy of any static inside it -- serves the process.
 * `local: *` unmerges them and our compilation of a SLATE inline then runs
 * against libslate's data structures.  Those duplicates are ODR-CORRECT
 * (same headers, same source); LORRAX's own were not.
 *
 * So the rule this file states is the rule the defect actually needs:
 * **LORRAX's own definitions are private to the library that compiled them.**
 * Everything else keeps the linkage the C++ ABI intends.
 *
 * A DENYLIST STILL FAILS SAFE, which is the other half of the choice.  A new
 * LORRAX symbol lands in `namespace lorrax_ffi` and is hidden automatically;
 * a new third-party dependency is left alone automatically.  The allowlist
 * form has the opposite failure mode -- forgetting a third-party namespace
 * does not fail the build, it segfaults at runtime, which is what the table
 * above is a record of.
 *
 * WHAT REMAINS SHARED, AND WHERE IT IS TRACKED.  ~234 third-party
 * vague-linkage names (slate::, blas::, xla::ffi::, libstdc++ internals) are
 * still defined by both files, and the FIRST library loaded still answers
 * them for both.  That is the SAME defect `libslate.so.2` / `libblaspp.so.2`
 * sharing one SONAME already is, it is what `_open_cuda_before_host` in both
 * loaders exists for, and `test_so_acceptance.py`'s check 5 ratchets it.
 * It is NOT L1 and this file does not claim to fix it.
 *
 * NOT -fvisibility=hidden, for a second and independent reason: gcc marks a
 * non-default-visibility type's typeinfo NAME private with a leading '*',
 * after which libstdc++'s `type_info::operator==` compares POINTERS ONLY
 * (typeinfo's `__name[0] != '*'` guard).  libslate.so throws
 * `slate::Exception` / `slate::MpiException` / `blas::Error` ACROSS the .so
 * boundary into our handlers, where they match today by strcmp on that name.
 * A version script relocalises symbols at LINK time without touching what
 * the compiler emitted, so the exception path is bit-for-bit unchanged.
 *
 * The FROZEN `*Ffi` handler symbols (invariant 1) and the `lrx_*` ctypes ABI
 * are not mentioned here at all: they are not `lorrax_ffi`-namespaced, so
 * they keep default visibility by not matching.  The nine `lrx_*` entry
 * points the HOST leg also compiles are disambiguated by RENAMING rather
 * than by hiding -- Python has to dlsym them -- see cpp/common/c_abi.h.
 */
{
  global:
    /* THE TWO EXCEPTIONS, and the reason this file grew a `global:` clause.
       Until 2026-08-08 the device leg carried no build stamp at all, so
       cpp/common/build_config.cc was host-only and nothing it defined could
       match the denylist below.  It is now in LORRAX_FFI_SOURCES too, and its
       two entry points are named `lorrax_ffi_cuda_*` — which the denylist
       would take.  An exact name beats a wildcard in a version script.

       Both are declared as APIs and both are read from OUTSIDE this library:
       the stamp by `strings`/the acceptance tier, the ABI by both Python
       loaders, at dlopen, before a single handler is declared.  They collide
       with nothing: the host leg's twins carry `_host`, not `_cuda`, so the
       two sanctioned surfaces stay disjoint and check 6's intersection stays
       empty.  Hiding them would not fail anything — it would silently disarm
       the pairing refusal, which is the failure mode
       cpp/common/lorrax_ffi_abi.h exists to remove. */
    lorrax_ffi_cuda_build_config;
    lorrax_ffi_cuda_abi_version;
  local:
    /* every C++ entity in `namespace lorrax_ffi`, including std:: templates
       instantiated on our types, our function-local statics (`_ZZ...`) and
       their guard variables (`_ZGVZ...`). */
    *lorrax_ffi*;
    /* the extern "C" CUDA kernel launchers in cublasmp/w_solve_kernels.cu.
       Cross-TU WITHIN this library (batched_w_solve_ffi.cc calls them) and
       reachable from nowhere else, so C linkage is an implementation detail
       here, not an interface. */
    lorrax_zero_upper_tri_*;
    lorrax_identity_minus_T_*;
};
